== dup_blocks_cross_pkg ==
dup_blocks_cross_pkg (v1, gated by default)
Definition: Duplicate blocks shared with other packages in the module: exact normalized repeats of at least duplication.min_tokens found over one module-wide stream, counted once per package they touch. The module-level row (package path "<module>") counts the distinct blocks and is baselined like a package, because one edit that copies code across packages changes two packages' counts. A rule on it is gated on the module row only: package rows report their count but are not gated on it, so one cross-package copy is one finding.
Evidence: Copy-paste instead of extraction: industry reports on AI-assisted repositories show rising duplicated blocks. The most specific LLM failure mode found; moderate evidence. The default gates the module row: max_delta 0, and max 450 (the corpus p90) for a module with no baseline. Against a git baseline, which records the blocks, the rule judges the baseline's count plus the blocks the change added: new blocks of at least duplication.min_tokens_cross tokens (default 80, near where golangci-lint's dupl starts reporting copies), not counting a block every package of which came out of one rename, the code a split leaves shared between its halves; the finding names only those, and the row keeps the full count. Against a baseline file, which records only the count, the full count is judged.
Thresholds in the loaded config: none
-- structured --
{
  "definition": "Duplicate blocks shared with other packages in the module: exact normalized repeats of at least duplication.min_tokens found over one module-wide stream, counted once per package they touch. The module-level row (package path \"\u003cmodule\u003e\") counts the distinct blocks and is baselined like a package, because one edit that copies code across packages changes two packages' counts. A rule on it is gated on the module row only: package rows report their count but are not gated on it, so one cross-package copy is one finding.",
  "evidence": "Copy-paste instead of extraction: industry reports on AI-assisted repositories show rising duplicated blocks. The most specific LLM failure mode found; moderate evidence. The default gates the module row: max_delta 0, and max 450 (the corpus p90) for a module with no baseline. Against a git baseline, which records the blocks, the rule judges the baseline's count plus the blocks the change added: new blocks of at least duplication.min_tokens_cross tokens (default 80, near where golangci-lint's dupl starts reporting copies), not counting a block every package of which came out of one rename, the code a split leaves shared between its halves; the finding names only those, and the row keeps the full count. Against a baseline file, which records only the count, the full count is judged.",
  "gated": true,
  "name": "dup_blocks_cross_pkg",
  "release": "v1",
  "thresholds": []
}

== instability ==
instability (v1, not gated by default)
Definition: Martin instability Ce / (Ca + Ce) with Ca = fan_in and Ce = internal_imports; 0 is maximally stable, 1 maximally unstable. Null when both are 0.
Evidence: Reported, not gated. Martin's package metrics are widely reported but their validation as predictors is mixed and none exists for Go, whose consumer-defined interfaces invert the abstract-provider assumption: idiomatic Go puts stable concrete leaf packages near distance 1 by design. No gate rule until the reference-corpus measurement shows where well-regarded Go modules sit.
Thresholds in the loaded config: none
-- structured --
{
  "definition": "Martin instability Ce / (Ca + Ce) with Ca = fan_in and Ce = internal_imports; 0 is maximally stable, 1 maximally unstable. Null when both are 0.",
